The XIAOML Kit with ESP32S3 Sense is an accessible entry point to TinyML and embedded machine learning. Through this setup process, we have tested every component of the XIAOML Kit, confirming that all sensors and peripherals are functioning correctly. The ESP32S3’s dual-core processor and 8 MB of PSRAM provide sufficient computational power for real-time ML inference, while the OV2640 camera, digital microphone, LSM6DS3TR-C IMU, and 0.42” OLED display form a multimodal sensing platform. WiFi connectivity allows edge-to-cloud ML workflows, and the Arduino IDE development environment is now configured with the necessary libraries.
Beyond the functionality tests, we have gained practical insight into the coordinate systems, data formats, and operating characteristics of each sensor. This knowledge will help when designing ML data collection and preprocessing pipelines for the upcoming projects.
This setup process demonstrates principles that apply beyond this kit. Working within the ESP32S3’s memory and processing limits gives direct experience of the resource constraints of edge AI, the same constraints that apply when deploying models on smartphones, IoT devices, or autonomous systems. Having multiple modalities (vision, audio, motion) on a single platform enables exploration of multimodal ML approaches, which are increasingly important in real-world AI applications.
From raw sensor data to model inference to user feedback on the OLED display, the kit covers a complete ML deployment cycle in miniature, mirroring the challenges of production AI systems.
With this foundation in place, you are ready for the core TinyML applications in the following chapters:

Vision Projects: Using the camera for image classification and object detection
Audio Projects: Processing audio streams for keyword spotting and voice recognition
Motion Projects: Using IMU data for activity recognition and anomaly detection

Each application will build upon the hardware understanding and software infrastructure we have established, showing how artificial intelligence can be deployed not only in data centers but also in resource-constrained devices that directly interact with the physical world.
The principles encountered with this kit (real-time processing, sensor fusion, and edge inference) also apply to AI deployment in autonomous vehicles, smart cities, medical devices, and industrial automation. With this setup complete, you are prepared to explore embedded machine learning.




