This hands-on lab continues the exploration of AI applications at the edge, from the basic setup of Florence-2, Microsoft’s vision foundation model, to implementations on devices like the Raspberry Pi. You will learn to use Vision Language Models (VLMs) for tasks such as captioning, object detection, grounding, segmentation, and OCR on a Raspberry Pi.


Florence-2 is a vision-language model open-sourced by Microsoft under the MIT license, which combines a lightweight architecture with broad capabilities. Because it was trained on the FLD-5B dataset, which contains 126 million images and 5.4 billion visual annotations, it achieves performance comparable to larger models. This suits Florence-2 to deployment at the edge, where power and computational resources are limited.
This tutorial shows how to use Florence-2 for computer vision applications, such as:

Image captioning
Object detection
Segmentation
Visual grounding


Visual grounding involves linking textual descriptions to specific regions within an image. This enables the model to understand where particular objects or entities described in a prompt are in the image. For example, if the prompt is “a red car,” the model will identify and highlight the region where the red car is found in the image. Visual grounding helps in applications where precise alignment between text and visual content is needed, such as human-computer interaction, image annotation, and interactive AI systems.

The tutorial covers:

Setting up Florence-2 on the Raspberry Pi
Running inference tasks such as object detection and captioning
Optimizing the model for the edge device
Applying fine-tuning to practical, real-world applications.




Florence-2 uses a unified, prompt-based representation to handle various vision-language tasks. The model architecture consists of two main components: an image encoder and a multi-modal transformer encoder-decoder.
